{
  "result": "The morning light arrives quietly over the town, spilling across rooftops and catching in the windows of small houses that have not yet decided to wake up. I make coffee and carry it to the porch, where the air smells of rain and cut grass and something else I cannot quite name. A dog trots past without purpose, and I wonder where he is going, what important business could possibly fill such a small head at this hour. My neighbor waves from her garden, holding a trowel like a tiny sword, and I wave back with my free hand, still warm around the mug. There is no real reason to hurry today, and that feels like a small gift handed to me without ceremony. The river beyond the road moves slowly, carrying leaves, reflections, and pieces of sky toward someplace I will never see. I think about all the ordinary days that people forget, the Tuesdays and quiet Sundays that slip away while everyone waits for something bigger to happen. Maybe this is the bigger thing, this unremarkable morning with its coffee, its dog, and its neighbor waving like she means it. I sit a little longer, watching the light climb higher, and I decide to write down nothing more than this feeling, this brief and complete sense that the world is enough exactly as it is. Then I will go inside, wash the cup, and begin whatever ordinary work is waiting for me downstairs today. That is perfectly fine.",
